> - **Look at every screenshot you rely on.** Open the file with `Read` and inspect it: layout, truncation, contrast, hierarchy. The accessibility tree alone does not show what the user sees.
> - **Compare before and after every state change.** Something that appears in one frame, content that shifts, a dialog that re-centers when its height changes: each is a finding (the values to fix it belong to `design-engineer`).
> - **Sweep the width** of the primary surface from 320 to 1280 in about 40 px steps, and screenshot where the layout changes: wraps, row limits and truncation break between breakpoints, not at them.
> - **Move the trigger.** Open overlays with their trigger near the top, the middle and the bottom of the viewport.
> - **axe.** Run `agent-browser a11y` when your CLI version has it. Otherwise inject axe-core with `eval`: from the project's `node_modules/axe-core/axe.min.js` when installed, else a pinned CDN build when the page can reach the network. Then run `axe.run()` **in every state you force** (step 3), not only the default view: limits, errors and success states carry their own colors and names. Violations become **one bundled list of mechanical fixes**, one line each with its location. `incomplete` items become manual checks. If neither route works, say the automated checks did not run.
